{#
 # SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
 # Copyright (c) 2026 Abdelmonem Awad <eg2@live.com>
 #
 # Application Control's Suricata rules: the rules of its Rules tab, for OPNsense's own Intrusion
 # Detection. scripts/appcontrol/suricata_rules.py renders this into
 # /usr/local/etc/suricata/rules/appcontrol.rules, which OPNsense's installRules.py installs beside its own
 # user rules. configd does not render it, and this folder has no +TARGETS: what the lines match - the
 # names the installed nDPI knows an application by, the addresses a source holds now in pf and on the
 # interfaces - is no setting of config.xml's. The script writes each line's matching options (one name in
 # one place a device writes it, or the application's protocol) and its sid; the shape of a line is here.
 #
 #   rules       [{number, action, app, verb (drop or alert), source and destination (Suricata's addresses:
 #                 never the firewall itself, the destination the internet alone), source_text, msg,
 #                 lines: [{proto, match, sid}]}]
 #   threshold   one alert a device a minute for each line; a drop still drops every packet it matches
 #}
# Application Control (os-appcontrol): the rules of its Rules tab, written by its page's Apply and kept
# current by its classifier. Change them there: this file is written again. A change made to one of these lines
# by its sid on the Intrusion Detection's page stays with that line while its rule stands.
{% for rule in rules %}
# rule {{ rule.number }}: {{ rule.action }} {{ rule.app }} from {{ rule.source_text }}
{% for line in rule.lines %}
{{ rule.verb }} {{ line.proto }} {{ rule.source }} any -> {{ rule.destination }} any (msg:"{{ rule.msg }}"; flow:to_server; {{ line.match }} {{ threshold }} sid:{{ line.sid }}; rev:1;)
{% endfor %}
{% endfor %}
